### Can you reuse a stream after a terminal operation?

No. After any terminal operation, the stream is consumed and throws IllegalStateException on any subsequent use.

### What happens when you try to reuse?

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.util.stream.Stream;

public class StreamReuseError {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Stream<String> stream = Stream.of("SAV2001", "SAV2002", "CUR3001");
        long count = stream.count(); // terminal #1: consumed
        System.out.println("Count: " + count);

        try {
            stream.toList(); // THROWS!
        } catch (IllegalStateException e) {
            System.out.println("Error: " + e.getMessage());
        }
    }
}
```

```text
Count: 3
Error: stream has already been operated upon or closed
```

### What is the Supplier workaround?

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.function.Supplier;
import java.util.stream.Stream;

public class SupplierWorkaround {
    record Account(String id, BigDecimal balance, boolean active) {}

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Account> accounts = List.of(
            new Account("SAV2001", new BigDecimal("125000"), true),
            new Account("SAV2002", new BigDecimal("8000"), false),
            new Account("CUR3001", new BigDecimal("250000"), true)
        );

        // Store the RECIPE, not the stream:
        Supplier<Stream<Account>> activeAccounts =
            () -> accounts.stream().filter(Account::active);

        // Each .get() creates a FRESH stream:
        long count = activeAccounts.get().count();
        List<Account> list = activeAccounts.get().toList();
        BigDecimal total = activeAccounts.get()
            .map(Account::balance)
            .reduce(BigDecimal.ZERO, BigDecimal::add);

        System.out.println("Count: " + count);
        System.out.println("Total: Rs." + total);
    }
}
```

```text
Count: 2
Total: Rs.375000
```

### What is the collect-first alternative?

```java
// Materialise once:
List<Account> activeList = accounts.stream()
    .filter(Account::active)
    .toList();

// Reuse the List (not the stream):
long count = activeList.size();
BigDecimal total = activeList.stream()
    .map(Account::balance)
    .reduce(BigDecimal.ZERO, BigDecimal::add);
```

### When should you use which?

| Approach | Source changes? | Memory | Lazy? |
|----------|----------------|--------|-------|
| Supplier | Reflects latest | Low (no copy) | Yes |
| Collect-first | Snapshot | Materialised list | No |

### What should a senior engineer know?

The single-use nature of streams is a design choice that enables efficient resource management. File-backed streams (Files.lines) hold OS file handles that are released when the stream is consumed. Allowing reuse would require either keeping the handle open indefinitely or re-opening the file, both of which have problems. The Supplier pattern cleanly separates the pipeline definition from its execution.
